# rail_plate_offset.cam — a valve-body-style rail plate: the LAND, and nothing else
# (Phase 30; DECISIONS.md → "Phase 30 slice 3: the rail plate…").
#
# 120 x 160, and the surface to finish is the tops of a network of RAILS 3mm
# wide — 4188mm² of land on a 19200mm² plate, 22% — with R5 bosses and O4 bolt
# holes where the rails meet. Every rail top is coplanar. It is all one
# surface, so a raster over the whole plate with a big cutter does it, 78% of
# it over nothing. And it is one or two walls away from a nightmare: raise any
# neighbour of any rail and that rail's edge is CLOSED.
#
# Today every edge is OPEN: the channels between the rails are below the land
# and nothing stands in them. Three faces, because a Face's inner loops may not
# nest and two rails stand free inside channel pockets (geometry.part).
#
# The tool is 3.3 rails wide. No pattern here gets to think in stepovers: a
# rail is covered by ONE pass anywhere within 3.5mm of its centreline, and the
# whole question is which passes, how long, and how many lifts between them.
units mm
feed 800
plunge 300
rapid_z 5
clearance_z 25
spindle 3000

# The plate, 1mm proud of the finished land, 2mm of margin all round.
stock Box(w=124, l=164, h=21, x=-2, y=-2, z=1)

tool T5 Endmill(dia=10.0)

run End(tool=T5, surface=Land, stepover=5.0, pattern=offset)
run End(tool=T5, surface=RingRail, stepover=5.0, pattern=offset)
run End(tool=T5, surface=BarRail, stepover=5.0, pattern=offset)
